1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
LabGuard AI — an autonomous research reliability platform.
Challenge the claim. Protect the run. Trust the result.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:11,500
An experiment that ran correctly can still support the wrong
conclusion. LabGuard checks both sides.

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:17,000
It ships with a scenario, but here I'll submit
a claim of my own.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
LabGuard rebuilds a synthetic benchmark with the shape I describe
and trains these configurations itself.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The dataset: 4,000 rows, 24 features,
8% positive — a heavily imbalanced detection task.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The baseline: a class-weighted linear model,
25 epochs.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:47,000
The candidate: an MLP with 24 hidden units,
trained for 90 epochs. Already an unequal budget.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:56,000
And the result I already have — reported on a single seed,
with the checkpoint chosen on the test split.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Every one of those choices is something
LabGuard will challenge.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:12,000
The claim: our model beats the MIL model
at identifying threats in violence detection.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
That is the whole submission. From here
the platform runs on its own.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The Claim Analyst starts immediately.
The claim is now under verification.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Note the header: this is running on Cloud Run, against Firestore
and Pub/Sub, reasoning with Gemini 3.5 Flash through Google ADK.

14
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Overall claim confidence, and the arithmetic behind it:
0.6 times the dimension mean, plus 0.4 times the evidence balance.

15
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Seven reliability dimensions, each a weighted pass rate
over named checks. No score here is written by a model.

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
The dataset and both configurations are recorded,
so the whole verification can be reproduced.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The Claim map. The Scientific Skeptic has taken over,
turning one sentence into testable subclaims.

18
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Then the Experiment Planner decides
what to actually run.

19
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Round one: four diagnostics for 7.4 compute units, chosen to target
configuration discrepancies, train/test overlap and class imbalance.

20
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:28,000
The queue. Three jobs published, one already running —
over Pub/Sub, to a separate Cloud Run worker.

21
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Nothing runs inline. The orchestrator publishes approved work
and the worker picks it up asynchronously.

22
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The evidence ledger. Append-only: every row records who acted,
why, what they were given, what came back, and what they decided.

23
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:53,000
I can filter it by agent — here, everything
the Scientific Skeptic did and its reasoning.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
This is the audit trail. Every conclusion
traces back to a measurement.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Back on the overview, the scores update
as each piece of evidence lands.

26
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Seven agents on Gemini 3.5 Flash through Google ADK, sharing state
in Firestore, with Pub/Sub and three Cloud Run services.
